WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:05.450
USGS counts major earthquakes,
magnitude 7 to 7.9.

2
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.770
In 1989 there were 6.

3
00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:09.790
In 2010, 23.

4
00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.670
Year-to-year swings like that are normal.

5
00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.390
There is no long-run upward trend.

6
00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:22.030
Yet recorded earthquake disasters
rose, from 445 between 1980 and 1999,

7
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:26.790
to 552 between 2000 and 2019.

8
00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:28.650
Is Earth becoming more active?

9
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:30.730
Is it just better reporting?

10
00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.910
Why do experts expect more
people to be affected?

11
00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.290
In this mission, you'll test these trends,

12
00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:40.690
then turn to adaptation, starting with
zoning that keeps buildings off active

13
00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:41.670
faults.

14
00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:44.810
You'll also weigh insurance,
drills like ShakeOut,

15
00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:48.690
and phone alerts sent seconds to
minutes before strong shaking.

16
00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:52.950
Your mission: explain why experts
expect more people to be affected,

17
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.410
even though big earthquakes
aren't increasing.
